+# Senate written answers on NORAD review and discipline
+
+Public derivative of the November 10, 2026 source review. B129 / PD01–PD04. The Senate Armed Services Committee hearing volume records a request for a written response on the accuracy of NORAD’s earlier testimony, a written answer about an after-action review, and a written position on discipline. A separate index names three potentially relevant report ranges. The original Senate routing and clearance records, report bodies and actual FAA decisions remain open. [S. Hrg. 108–875](https://www.govinfo.gov/app/details/CHRG-108shrg24495/CHRG-108shrg24495)
+
+## The congressional record
+
+| Event or answer | What the published record preserves |
+| --- | --- |
+| August 17, 2004 oral exchange | Senator Mark Dayton requested a response in writing about discrepancies in NORAD’s May 2003 testimony and its statement from the week after the attacks. He raised possible misrepresentation. General Richard Myers replied orally that accounts can change as facts and recordings are reviewed and challenged an inference of deceptive motive. The volume gives the hearing dates **August 16 and 17, 2004** and the printing year **2005**. [PDF pp. 156–157, printed pp. 153–154](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-108shrg24495/pdf/CHRG-108shrg24495.pdf#page=156) |
+| Question **31** | The question names Secretary Donald Rumsfeld. The printed answer is attributed to **General Myers**. It says an investigation into the decision about forwarding presidential engagement authority to the pilots was deemed unnecessary and was therefore not conducted. Question 30 separately concerns what was relayed to the pilots. The written answer to question 31 addresses that particular decision. [PDF p. 180, printed p. 177, extracted lines 45–59](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-108shrg24495/pdf/CHRG-108shrg24495.pdf#page=180) |
+| Question **40** | Senators Dayton and Hillary Rodham Clinton asked whether there was an investigation or after-action review, and asked for its date, officer and a written copy if conducted. The printed answer attributed to **Secretary Rumsfeld** reads, “The Department of Defense did not conduct an after-action review regarding NORAD’s actions on September 11, 2001.” He described NORAD’s document and personal-account production to the Commission and changes to air-defense arrangements. The written answer gives no individual report titles or transmittal date. [PDF p. 184, printed p. 181, extracted lines 37–58](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-108shrg24495/pdf/CHRG-108shrg24495.pdf#page=184) |
+| Question **41** | The printed answer attributed to **General Myers** places accountability with the military chain of command and states that “no disciplinary measures are warranted.” It cites mission posture, existing engagement guidance, late notification, time and distance. That is the published Defense position on NORAD’s operational response. The signed decision, authorization and any notifications or later updates remain unacquired. [PDF pp. 184–185, printed pp. 181–182](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-108shrg24495/pdf/CHRG-108shrg24495.pdf#page=184) |
+
+The question numbers identify a concrete Senate Armed Services Committee packet to seek. The oral request about the accuracy of statements and questions 40–41 about NORAD’s actions and accountability have distinct wording. The hearing publication establishes that the printed answers entered the public hearing record. A cover, tracking entry or enclosure list is needed to determine when they were sent, what was attached, and whether a separate response specifically addressed the accuracy request.
+
+## Preserve the descriptions of after-action work side by side
+
+- The February 17, 2004 Commission interview memo records that Myers had seen after-action **analysis** about FAA notifications, while distinguishing it from what he would call a report. It separately records a request to determine if a review had been conducted and a commitment to contact NORAD after discussion of Flight 93. [NARA interview memo, pp. 6–7](https://www.archives.gov/files/declassification/iscap/pdf/2012-047-doc4.pdf#page=6)
+- The March 16, 2004 NORAD legal memorandum distinguishes a single final comprehensive report from a **series** of lessons-learned reports it says went to the Commission. The forwarding DoD counsel letter says material it considered responsive was listed in weekly delivery indexes. Those are sender accounts. [Prior verified March correspondence review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/3324e3ae36db9189a79658db51ef04acb6663219/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-march16-after-action-correspondence-2026-11-08/README.md)
+- The Senate answer attributed to Rumsfeld gives the statement in question 40 above. Keep the Senate wording, interview memo and March correspondence as separate records. The tasking, definitions actually used to prepare the Senate answer, and the underlying bodies and delivery indexes would let us test the relationship. [Question 40](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-108shrg24495/pdf/CHRG-108shrg24495.pdf#page=184)
+
+The previously preserved public-reader copy of the Commission DoD index supplies three new exact retrieval ranges for this comparison. Its rightmost dates are preserved as printed fields. They do not independently establish creation or actual receipt.
+
+| Exact Bates range | Public-reader title or label | Rightmost field | Reader text line |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| **NCT0016329–NCT0016343** | NORAD Interim Joint AAR, **12 Mar 02 -11 Sep 02**. A repeated text row renders the endpoint **1 1 Sep 02**. | **25-Jul-03** | **1351, 1767, 1912** |
+| **NCT0025873–NCT0025919** | **After Action Report**, office field **CONR** | **12-Sep-03** | **1526** |
+| **NCT0033267–NCT0033374** | Op Noble Eagle Norad Interim Joint After-Action Rpt (JAAR) | **22-Oct-03** | **788** |
+
+The duplicated first range represents one retrieval identifier range across three text rows. The inclusive counts are **15**, **47** and **108** identifiers. They are search bounds. The native index faces and the three report bodies remain unacquired. The similar interim titles leave any edition, overlap or attachment relationship open. The CONR row gives no subject beyond its title, so test its event scope from the cover and actual contents. [Public reader, DoD index](https://www.scribd.com/document/15556058/DM-B3-DOD-2-of-2-Fdr-All-Document-Indexes-in-File-272)
+
+## What would advance the FAA inquiry
+
+- [ ] **Defense counterpart.** Seek the original dated questions and signed replies for 31, 40 and 41, their approval and receipt, and any separate written answer to Dayton’s oral request. Compare the action record to the printed attribution and the scope actually answered.
+- [ ] **After-action records.** Start with the CONR index entry **NCT0025873–NCT0025919**, marked **Unclas**, then seek the native pages for the two interim report entries, and the named weekly delivery and Commission intake records. Test event scope, authorship, report dates, enclosure identities and overlap with Myers’s February analysis. Keep the March chronology letter and September 2006 Defense OIG testimony review in their separate chains. [Previously recovered Defense OIG memorandum](https://web.archive.org/web/20120707002543id_/http://www.dodig.mil/Ir/reports/06-intel-12.pdf)
+- [ ] **FAA PD01–PD04.** The sought FAA records are still the May 2003 corrected answer and recipient acknowledgment, the signed consideration or action/no-action decision, the recommendation-specific disposition, and the case-specific policy/routing record. The Defense answer concerns NORAD operational accountability. It does not determine how the FAA responded to the later recommendation about its executives’ handling of the inaccurate answer. [DOT OIG recommendations 1 and 2, p. 12](https://www.oig.dot.gov/sites/default/files/cc2006085.pdf#page=12)
+
+Both GovInfo bodies were downloaded on November 10 UTC. PDF and HTML preserve the same hearing publication in two representations. Verification checked 16 fixed PDF anchors across six pages and seven raw HTML line anchors. The cited areas of the six PDF pages were visually inspected. The five index text rows were reproduced from the previously held HTML and checked by Bates range. The wider hearing, native index faces, original report bodies and disposition files were outside the verified reading scope.
